course: dictionary
lesson: 14005
title: Luminosity: how much light something gives out
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Luminosity is a science word for how much light something gives out. It means how bright the thing is in itself. «The lamp has a high luminosity, so it lights up the whole room.»
explain: Looking bright and being bright are not always the same. «The star looks faint from Earth, but its luminosity is very high.» It is truly giving out a lot of light, but it is so far away that very little of it reaches our eyes.
explain: Think about the thing making the light. A torch, a light bulb or a star gives out its own light, so we can measure its luminosity. A screen on a phone or TV is also a light source, and in everyday talk we usually call its luminosity brightness.
fact: Luminosity is how much light something gives out.
fact: High luminosity means something gives out a lot of light; low luminosity means it gives out only a little.
fact: In astronomy, the luminosity of a star is the total energy it gives out each second.
fact: A star can look faint from Earth and still have high luminosity, because it is very far away.
fact: In everyday life, brightness is the more common word for the light from phone and TV screens.
exercise: «The torch has a high luminosity.» What does high luminosity mean? | It is very heavy | It is very cold | It gives out very little light | *It gives out a lot of light
exercise: «The star looks faint, but its luminosity is very high.» What does this tell you about the star? | It is only a reflection of the Sun | It is small and cool | *It is truly very bright, but it is far away | It has no light at all
exercise: Which everyday word is often used for the light from a phone or TV screen? | *Brightness | Length | Silence | Weight
test: «The lamp's luminosity was low, so the room felt dim.» What does low luminosity mean? | It gives out a lot of heat | It makes a loud noise | *It gives out only a little light
test: In astronomy, what does the luminosity of a star measure? | *The total energy it gives out each second | Its size on a map | How close it is to Earth | Its colour only
exam: Why can a star with high luminosity look faint from Earth? | It has no light at all | *It is very far away | It is made of ice | It is only a shadow
